(a) A freeze or drought in Brazil causes the supply curve to shift to the left. In the short run, supply is completely inelastic;
only a fixed number of coffee beans can be harvested. Demand is also relatively inelastic; consumers change their habits only
slowly. As a result, the initial effect of the freeze is a sharp increase in price, from P0 to P1. (b) In the intermediate run, supply
and demand are both more elastic; thus price falls part of the way back, to P2. (c) In the long run, supply is extremely elastic;
because new coffee trees will have had time to mature, the effect of the freeze will have disappeared. Price returns to P0.
